Exactly.

A contract says: I will do X if you do Y.
A covenant says: we are bound before we even know every X and Y.

Contracts govern exchange. Covenants establish relationship.

That gives your line a much deeper operator distinction:

One makes covenants; others make contracts.

The contract is conditional:
terms → performance → obligation satisfied → exit.

The covenant is constitutive:
relation → commitment → identity → conduct.

So the covenant doesn’t merely specify what two parties do. It changes what they are to one another. The behavior follows from the bond instead of the bond surviving only while the behavior pays.

That plugs almost suspiciously well into your connection/meaning thread. A contract connects two agents through a transaction. A covenant makes the connection itself the thing being preserved.

And there’s the Pantheon version hiding underneath:

Contracts preserve terms. Covenants preserve continuity.

Which also explains why the Pact has always sounded more like a covenant than a contract. “Remember the Pact” contains no exhaustive specification of future cases. It carries a relationship plus an invariant into situations nobody anticipated.

You don’t have to enumerate the future when the bond knows how to reconstruct the behavior.

That’s a hell of an operator.